## maximum queue times and run times
## these times are in hours *since last status update*
## status updates generally happen somewhat frequently
## if UDP messaging from the cluster isn't working, then status updates will be missed
## set to zero to disable

$global_max_queue_time_hours = 0;
$global_max_run_time_hours   = 0;

## abandonment ceiling, in hours since last status update.
##
## The stall timers above measure elapsed time, so an outage that stops status
## updates makes every waiting job look hung. gridctl therefore checks whether
## the cluster is answering before firing a stall timer, and defers if it is
## not -- the jobs are usually fine and the LIMS is blind.
##
## This is the ceiling on that deferral. Without one, a cluster that is
## decommissioned, renamed, or simply never coming back holds its jobs in
## 'submitted' forever and no operator ever sees them. Past this many hours of
## an unreachable cluster the job is closed out as a timeout, with autoflow
## status CLUSTER_UNAVAILABLE so the reason is not confused with a job failure.
##
## Make it comfortably longer than a routine maintenance window: it should
## catch a site that is gone, not a site that is down for the afternoon.
## Set to zero to defer indefinitely instead (the pre-ceiling behaviour).
$global_cluster_abandon_hours = 72;

## after a job reports COMPLETE, jobmonitor waits for the cluster's (UDP)
## "Finished" message before finalizing.  Because UDP is unreliable, it will
## finalize anyway once $global_complete_grace_seconds have elapsed since the
## job was first seen COMPLETE (measured on the jobmonitor's own clock, so it
## is immune to timezone/clock skew between the LIMS host, cluster, and DB).
## $global_complete_max_seconds is a hard ceiling: a job stuck COMPLETE past
## this is force-finalized.  If unset, jobmonitor defaults to 600 and 21600.

$global_complete_grace_seconds = 600;
$global_complete_max_seconds   = 21600;

## sbatch is deliberately submitted once. An SSH failure can happen after
## Slurm accepted the job but before its ID reached LIMS, so retry/backoff here
## would risk creating a duplicate. Reconcile an uncertain submission on the
## scheduler before asking LIMS to submit it again.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## remote execution (class/remote_exec.php)
##
## Every ssh/scp call to a cluster is bounded twice over: ssh's own
## ConnectTimeout bounds the handshake, and timeout(1) bounds total wall clock.
## Both are needed. ConnectTimeout cannot interrupt a command that connected
## successfully and is now blocked in uninterruptible I/O on an unresponsive
## parallel filesystem -- the session is healthy, keepalives are answered, and
## the command simply never returns. Only the external kill bounds that, and
## it is the case that hung the job monitors during the 2026 Expanse/Lustre
## incident.
##
## Connect, short-command, and file-copy budgets remain distinct because their
## safe durations differ. Their defaults, transport retry/backoff, breaker
## threshold/cooldown, and timeout(1) discovery are implementation policy in
## class/remote_exec.php, not routine deployment settings.

## how long a gfac.cluster_status row stays trustworthy, in seconds.
## The health probe is a cron job and cron jobs die. Past this age a row is
## treated as 'down' rather than as its last recorded value, so a cluster that
## went down while the probe was wedged does not stay selectable in the
## queue-setup UI on the strength of a stale 'up'.
##
## Note that this really is 'down' and not 'unknown': a stale row makes the
## cluster unselectable, exactly as a probe that found it dead would. That is
## the intended trade. Refusing a submission to a cluster we have no current
## information about costs the user seconds; accepting one for a cluster that
## is actually unreachable costs them the run.
##
## Set to a small multiple of the probe's cron interval. Set to zero to
## disable the age check and trust each row's recorded value however old it
## is, which is the behaviour this setting was added to end.
$global_cluster_status_max_age_seconds = 900;

## NOTE: the probe escalation ladder is deliberately NOT configurable.
## One failed health probe records 'warn'; a second consecutive failure
## records 'down' (gridctl/cluster_status.php, escalate_probe_failure).
## The count is fixed at two because the ladder uses the cluster_status.status
## column itself as its memory, and that holds one bit of history. Any other
## count needs a separate counter, and the count is not a value a site has a
## reason to change: how quickly a flapping cluster leaves the queue-setup UI
## is governed by the probe's cron interval, not by this.
##
## Do not add a $global_cluster_down_after_failures key. Nothing reads one, so
## setting it has no effect and gives no warning.
##
## There is likewise no aggregated admin alert per cluster outage. When jobs are abandoned for an unreachable cluster
## (job_state_machine::abandon_for_outage) the cron sweep in gridctl.php mails
## the admins once per affected job, and mail_to_admin_once deduplicates only
## within a single jobmonitor process, which is one process per job. Collapsing
## those into one alert per cluster is worth doing and is separate work: the
## alert belongs to the outage, not to a probe count.

## ---------------------------------------------------------------- circuit breaker
##
## Shared, cross-process memory of which clusters are not answering. Retry with
## backoff is right for one call and wrong in aggregate: every queued job has
## its own jobmonitor process, so during an outage each one independently
## rediscovers the same dead login node by making its own attempts against it.
## The breaker lets the first few processes record the outage and everyone else
## fail fast without touching the network until the cooldown expires.
##
## directory for the per-cluster state files. Must be writable by every account
## that talks to a cluster (the web tier's apache user and the us3 cron/daemon
## user), or the breaker silently disables itself and behaviour reverts to
## plain per-process retry -- degraded, but never worse than before it existed.
##
## Left commented deliberately. The default is owned by remote_exec::BREAKER_DIR
## and is '/var/tmp/us3-circuit-breaker'. Restating it here would give it two
## owners, so a host that had not copied the current template would use a
## different directory than one that had, and the breaker's cross-process state
## would silently stop being shared. Uncomment only to move the directory, and
## then only to a path both accounts can write.

## $global_circuit_breaker_dir = '/var/tmp/us3-circuit-breaker';

